# Supabase RLS And Migration Safety

Every migration that creates or exposes data in Supabase must handle access
control in the same file. Do not rely on a follow-up migration to make a table,
RPC, view, or storage path safe.

## Required For New Public Tables

Every `CREATE TABLE` in `public` must also include:

1. `ALTER TABLE public.<table> ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;`
2. At least one `CREATE POLICY ... FOR SELECT` policy, or a `FOR ALL` policy
   that covers reads.
3. Write policies for every intended write path.
4. Explicit `GRANT` statements when the table must be reachable through the
   Supabase Data API by `anon` or `authenticated`.

RLS controls rows after the table is reachable. It does not replace Data API
grants. Do not rely on broad historical schema defaults. Revoke unnecessary
`INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, `TRUNCATE`, `REFERENCES`, and `TRIGGER`
privileges, then grant only the operations used by a documented path.

Set safe default privileges for future objects as part of a privilege-hardening
migration. Table defaults must not silently give `anon` or `authenticated`
write or maintenance privileges, and function defaults must not expose new
helpers to `public`.

## Standard Policy Patterns

Use `TO authenticated` / `TO anon` instead of `auth.role()` checks. Treat
`TO authenticated` as authentication only; it is not authorisation unless it is
paired with an ownership or permission predicate.

### Per-user data

```sql
alter table public.<table> enable row level security;

create policy <table>_select_own
  on public.<table>
  for select
  to authenticated
  using ((select auth.uid()) = user_id);

create policy <table>_insert_own
  on public.<table>
  for insert
  to authenticated
  with check ((select auth.uid()) = user_id);

create policy <table>_update_own
  on public.<table>
  for update
  to authenticated
  using ((select auth.uid()) = user_id)
  with check ((select auth.uid()) = user_id);
```

Use this shape for owner-scoped tables such as `shows` and `song_analyses`.
AI credit ledger writes must use their guarded RPCs rather than direct table
mutations.

### Show-owned child rows

```sql
create policy <table>_select_via_show
  on public.<table>
  for select
  to authenticated
  using (
    exists (
      select 1
      from public.shows s
      where s.id = <table>.show_id
        and s.user_id = (select auth.uid())
    )
  );
```

Use this for tables like `show_timeline_items` that inherit ownership through
`shows`.

### Admin-managed reference data

```sql
create policy <table>_select_authenticated
  on public.<table>
  for select
  to authenticated
  using (true);

create policy <table>_admin_insert
  on public.<table>
  for insert
  to authenticated
  with check ((select public.current_user_has_permission('admin.manage_catalogue')));

create policy <table>_admin_update
  on public.<table>
  for update
  to authenticated
  using ((select public.current_user_has_permission('admin.manage_catalogue')))
  with check ((select public.current_user_has_permission('admin.manage_catalogue')));

create policy <table>_admin_delete
  on public.<table>
  for delete
  to authenticated
  using ((select public.current_user_has_permission('admin.manage_catalogue')));
```

Use command-specific write policies when a table also has a read policy. A
permissive `FOR ALL` policy also applies to `SELECT`, which creates a redundant
per-row policy plan. Wrap stable authorisation helpers in `select` so Postgres
initialises them once per statement. Use permission checks for admin tables.
Current permission keys include
`admin.view`, `admin.manage_users`, `admin.manage_billing`,
`admin.impersonate_users`, `admin.manage_suppliers`, `admin.manage_catalogue`,
`admin.manage_imports`, and `admin.manage_prompts`.

### Public browse data

Anonymous `SELECT` is intentional only when the data is public product or
template content. Current examples:

- `show_presets`
- `show_preset_like_counts`
- `catalogue_items`
- `fireworks`
- `firework_effects`
- `multishots`
- `multishot_fireworks`

When allowing anonymous reads:

```sql
grant select on public.<table> to anon;

-- Intentionally public: explain the browse surface that needs this.
create policy <table>_select_anyone
  on public.<table>
  for select
  to anon, authenticated
  using (true);
```

Add a SQL comment explaining the public surface, for example `/catalogue`,
`/library`, or `/home` template browsing.

If a public table also has admin-only columns, revoke the table-wide anonymous
grant and grant only the explicit public columns. In particular,
`show_presets.source_show_id` is provenance and must remain admin-only.

`show_preset_likes` is not public social data. Authenticated users may read only
their own row and must mutate likes through the guarded toggle RPC. Anonymous
visitors read the aggregate count table, which must not expose user identities.

## Security-definer RPCs

`SECURITY DEFINER` bypasses RLS. Use it only when the function is deliberately
the access boundary.

Every security-definer function must:

- Set `search_path = ''` and schema-qualify referenced objects.
- Check `auth.uid()` or `public.current_user_has_permission(...)` inside the
  function body.
- Be idempotent when it writes user-facing state.
- Revoke execute from `public`.
- Grant execute only to the exact role that needs it.
- Have a focused test for allowed and denied paths.

Trigger-only and internal helper functions should have execute revoked from
`public`, `anon`, and `authenticated`. If one security-definer function must
call another internal helper, grant that helper only to the owning database
role or replace the call with a checked public wrapper.

The AI credit RPCs and `replace_show_timeline_items` are examples of functions
that need explicit grants and caller checks. Never add `SECURITY DEFINER` just
to silence a permission error.

## Views

Views bypass RLS by default. In Postgres 15+, create exposed views with
`WITH (security_invoker = true)`. Otherwise keep views out of exposed schemas or
revoke access from `anon` and `authenticated`.

## Storage

Storage policies live on `storage.objects`, not the app table.

- The `audio` bucket is private and owner-scoped by the first path segment,
  `<user id>/...`.
- Audio and cover owner writes must also require a current active app user so a
  deleted or suspended account cannot reuse an unexpired access JWT.
- The `audio` bucket should keep the 50 MB limit and MP3, WAV, AAC, M4A MIME
  allow-list aligned with `/api/music-analysis`.
- The `import-videos` bucket is admin-only and is used by queued import jobs.
- Upsert-style storage writes need `INSERT`, `SELECT`, and `UPDATE`.

## Schema Integrity And Delivery

- Create migrations with `supabase migration new <name>` so timestamp prefixes
  remain unique and chronological. Never edit a migration that has reached a
  linked environment.
- Preflight existing rows before adding a check, unique index, `NOT NULL`, or
  foreign key. Repair data explicitly or fail with a useful assertion.
- Index foreign-key columns used for joins, cascades, ownership checks, or RLS.
  Do not retain a redundant index when a unique or primary-key index has the
  same useful leading columns.
- Use `source_show_id` as the nullable, unique provenance link for imported
  Explore presets. Do not infer identity from a title or slug after import.
- Keep new `show_presets` draft-first. Preset JSON writes use canonical
  `catalogueItemId` UUIDs; publication and cloning must reject unresolved,
  out-of-duration, or same-launch-position overlapping cues.
- Enforce the published-preset contract in Postgres as well as the server
  action, because a permitted Data API update can bypass application code.
- Regenerate `platform/lib/database.types.ts` after the migration is applied to
  the target schema. Review the type diff rather than hand-editing it.
- Before applying remotely, run the relevant tests, `supabase migration list
--linked`, and `supabase db push --linked --dry-run`. After applying, rerun
  the list, database lint, and focused read-only verification queries.

## Review Checklist

When reviewing a migration PR, confirm:

- [ ] New public tables enable RLS.
- [ ] New public tables have read policies and intended write policies.
- [ ] Ownership checks use `(select auth.uid())`, not user-editable metadata.
- [ ] Permission checks use `public.current_user_has_permission(...)`.
- [ ] Anonymous `SELECT` has a clear SQL comment explaining the public surface.
- [ ] Data API grants match the intended `anon` / `authenticated` access.
- [ ] Unused write, maintenance, and function execute privileges are revoked.
- [ ] Storage bucket and object policies are updated when storage behaviour
      changes.
- [ ] Security-definer functions revoke `public` execute and grant only the
      required role.
- [ ] Internal and trigger functions are not executable through the Data API.
- [ ] `platform/lib/database.types.ts` is regenerated when schema changes.
- [ ] Tests cover any changed access path or RPC behaviour.
- [ ] Foreign keys and common RLS/join paths have suitable indexes without
      redundant duplicates.
- [ ] Constraints were checked against existing data before enforcement.
- [ ] The migration filename prefix is unique across
      `platform/supabase/migrations/`.
